<p># The Last Mile of AI Is Not Technical. It's Human.</p>

<hr />

<h2>Why "the model isn't good enough" is usually the wrong answer</h2>

<p>When an AI initiative stalls, the reflex is to blame the technology. The model needs more data, a better benchmark, the next version. It's a comforting story, because it keeps the failure safely outside the organization.</p>

<p>But the numbers tell a different story.</p>

<p>McKinsey finds that roughly nine in ten companies have launched AI transformations, yet only about a quarter report meaningful results, and only around one in ten have scaled the genuine article — the other three-quarters are stuck in pilot purgatory. In China, a 2025 survey of 128 business leaders across 18 industries by Beijing News' Beike Finance found that 89.84% had deployed AI, but only 16.41% had set up a dedicated AI team, nearly half (47.66%) had never trained their employees, and 48.44% lacked any clear expectation of return on investment.</p>

<p>The technology got deployed. The people never caught up. That gap — not the model — is where adoption actually dies.</p>

<p>This article makes one argument, and then spends the rest of its length making that argument rigorous: **the first variable in AI adoption is not the technology, it is the people.** Where that claim comes from, how to act on it, and where it honestly stops applying.</p>

<hr />

<h2>The lens that ties it together: Human-AI Fit</h2>

<p>Before the frameworks and the case studies, it helps to name the idea under which everything else falls. We call it **Human-AI Fit**.</p>

<p>The premise is deceptively simple: technology produces value only when three things line up — the *person*, the *task*, and the *technology*. None of them matters in isolation. Swap in a stronger model and you have changed exactly one corner of the triangle; if the person lacks the capability or the will, or if the task doesn't genuinely need AI and the workflow hasn't been redesigned to receive it, the triangle collapses. A more useful way to hold it in your head is a product:</p>

<p>**Value = People × Task × Technology.**</p>

<p>Push any one factor toward zero, and the whole thing drops to zero.</p>

<p>Three textbook observations flow from this frame, and they land surprisingly close to the survey data.</p>

<p>First, **diffusion is a curve, not a switch.** Rogers' classic work on the diffusion of innovations showed that new tools move through a population in an S-curve, from innovators and early adopters to the early majority, late majority, and laggards. What makes people cross from one group to the next is not the objective strength of the tool — it's five *perceived* attributes: relative advantage, compatibility, complexity, trialability, and observability. Every one of those is about how a person *feels* about the change. A better model lowers the bar for innovators. It does almost nothing to pull the majority across. When you read "90% started, 25% succeeded," what you are really reading is an organization that crossed the start line but stalled mid-curve — and the five perception levers were never pulled.</p>

<p>Second, **resistance is about loss, not competence.** Kotter's eight-step change model — urgency, coalition, vision, communicating it, removing obstacles, short-term wins, consolidation, anchoring in culture — contains, notably, *no step called "buy a better tool."* Every step is about people. And the killer variable in organizational change is not that employees don't understand the technology; it's that they're afraid of what they'll lose — a job, a sense of control, a familiar rhythm. This is why "training" is not a perk but a direct hedge against resistance. The 47.66% of Chinese companies that skipped it didn't save a line-item; they planted a landmine.</p>

<p>Third, **trust has a gradient, and it runs the wrong way.** In a Prosci study of 1,107 practitioners, confidence in AI declines as you descend the hierarchy — executives are highly confident, frontline workers broadly skeptical. That gap is itself a symptom of failed fit: the top believes the triangle is aligned, while the frontline feels it in their bones that it isn't.</p>

<p>This third observation matters more than it looks. Because the real cost of AI adoption is rarely the license fee or the compute. It's the distance between an executive who sees "AI is live now" and a frontline worker who feels "my job just got harder, for someone else's benefit." That distance doesn't close with a better model.</p>

<hr />

<h2>From diagnosis to decision: a four-step method</h2>

<p>Naming the problem is not the same as fixing it. Here is what the insight looks like as an operating framework, in four ordered steps: **Diagnose → Re-own → Incentivize → Measure.** The order is the point. Most organizations leap straight to step three — incentives — without ever knowing where, exactly, the catch failed.</p>

<h3>Step 1 — Diagnose: pinpoint where adoption is actually stuck</h3>

<p>People resist or fail to adopt the technology for more than one reason. Four questions will locate the break; any question you can't answer is the gap.</p>

<ul>

<li>**Ownership:** Is there a *named human* accountable for whether AI actually gets used? (The Chinese data: only 16.41% have a dedicated team.)</li>

<li>**Capability:** Can the frontline use it, and dare to? (47.66% did no training.)</li>

<li>**Process:** Was the workflow redesigned *for* the new tool, or is it an old process wearing a new shell? (McKinsey's "no systematic business reinvention.")</li>

<li>**Measurement:** Has anyone defined what "paid back" means, who calculates it, and how often? (48.44% had no ROI expectation.)</li>

</li></ul>

<p>The action is a short "adoption audit" scored against these four questions. Don't buy a tool first. The veteran finance official's first lesson was that "government doesn't have an innovation problem; it has an adoption problem." Start by asking where adoption is stuck.</p>

<h3>Step 2 — Re-own: give adoption a fixed owner</h3>

<p>Create a dedicated **AI adoption owner** — not another AI engineer, not another tool. The distinction is the whole job: this person's output is not "the model runs," but "the frontline is actually using it and getting results." They own adoption rates, active usage, and workflow change; the technical metrics are only an indirect concern.</p>

<p>Resetting the frontline supervisor's role is where it gets real. The finance official put it bluntly: the frontline supervisor decides whether a change sticks. PwC's manufacturing research points the same way — 72% of manufacturers name "employees' comfort with existing systems" as the top barrier to major technology change, and 57% cite "lack of training and readiness." Whether a supervisor personally models the behavior, explains the *why*, and keeps setting expectations is the difference between early curiosity compounding into adoption or curdling into resistance.</p>

<p>The readiness test: within 90 days, can this person articulate which of the four diagnostic questions is their organization's real bottleneck?</p>

<h3>Step 3 — Incentivize: make using AI the easier, better job</h3>

<p>Turn "using AI" from an additional burden into a less painful, more valuable way to work — and point the incentives at the frontline, not just the executive suite.</p>

<p>Rogers' trialability and observability, translated into action, are three moves: give the frontline a near-zero-cost way to try the tool and allow exploration (PwC's finding — curiosity accelerates when people are allowed to explore); make the gains visible in terms a frontline worker can feel, not a slide deck; and reward the *supervisors who bring others along*, not just the individual stars. Adoption is a diffusion behavior; your incentive design should reward helping others catch it, not winning alone.</p>

<p>One caution: if incentives only track "hours of human labor saved" while ignoring *who absorbs the cost of redesigning the workflow*, you will keep producing exactly the people in the survey data who know the math but won't move forward.</p>

<h3>Step 4 — Measure: get the math right, so you can cut and double down</h3>

<p>Define the ROI formula before launch, and dodge two traps.</p>

<p>Trap A is *nobody calculates* — money spent with no accounting, the 48.44% with no ROI expectation. Trap B is *calculating wrong* — counting only the hours saved while omitting redesign, training, and the parallel-run period, producing a false "it's not worth it" verdict.</p>

<p>Three checks: Have you separated *efficiency gains* (doing the same thing faster) from *capability gains* (doing things you couldn't do at all)? Have you set a review cadence for ROI — 90 days, six months — instead of computing it once? And do you have an explicit *kill switch* — a rule for which pilots get stopped when ROI misses, and which get doubled down when it hits?</p>

<p>The math, gotten right, is what breaks the structural deadlock McKinsey describes: everyone trapped in the pilot, no one willing to step forward. This is the solution to "75% stuck in pilot."</p>

<p>Four steps, one sentence: **diagnose where it's stuck → give adoption an owner → make the frontline want it → let the numbers decide what lives.** In that order, not any other.</p>

<hr />

<h2>Three cases: what was caught — and what wasn't</h2>

<p>A single positive example is thin. Two contrasts, in opposite directions, make the point hold.</p>

<p>**The positive — a veteran finance official.** A senior public-finance leader oversaw the digitization of savings bonds. The result: tens of millions of dollars saved and delivery a year early. The counterintuitive core of his method: "Government doesn't have an innovation problem; it has an adoption problem." The leverage wasn't in which system they bought, but in making frontline people and processes actually *receive* the tool — a named owner, a reset supervisor role, and a relentlessly repeated explanation of purpose. This is step-two in the wild.</p>

<p>**The first contrast — manufacturing's frontline resistance.** In manufacturing the technical upside is unambiguous: BCG estimates AI can unlock productivity gains of more than 30%, roughly 25% machine-performance improvement, and roughly 25% automation of indirect labor. The bottleneck is elsewhere — PwC found 72% of manufacturers name "employees' comfort with existing systems" as their top barrier, and 57% cite inadequate training. And there's a subtlety: manufacturing AI — predictive maintenance, quality inspection, production scheduling — depends heavily on real-time frontline feedback and operating experience. Someone has to feed the operational context in and pull the feedback back out. Without that human-in-the-loop, the tool, however good, stays a fragile experiment instead of becoming a trusted capability in the line. This is the textbook case of *the technology is good enough, the people haven't caught it*: the number-one barrier isn't model accuracy, it's that employees know the old system and don't want to move.</p>

<p>**The second contrast — medicine's professional trust gap.** IBM Watson for Oncology (2011–2018) is one of the most widely reported AI failures: in real clinical settings its accuracy fell short and it failed to generalize from training data to new cases, and the project was eventually terminated. Alongside it, frontline researchers broadly display a "black-box distrust" — they trust systems they helped build or validate, and flatly don't trust black boxes from outside.</p>

<p>But read this one carefully. Medicine is a high-precision, high-risk, heavily regulated domain, and here "the people won't catch it" rests on hard, legitimate grounds: professional autonomy, patient safety, explainability. This is simultaneously a case of "people didn't adopt" and an entry point to the next section — because in some settings, *refusing to adopt is the rational call, not an organizational defect.*</p>

<hr />

<h2>Where the argument honestly stops</h2>

<p>An absolute claim is a cheap claim. "People are the key variable" has edges, and it's worth drawing them. In three kinds of settings, the binding constraint really is the technology or the foundation, not the people.</p>

<p>**Small-data settings.** Generative AI's strength depends on abundant, high-quality data. When an organization's data is sparse, sparsely labeled, or the domain is cold, the model has nothing to generalize from — and no amount of adoption will substitute for "nothing to learn." Niche production lines, low-resource languages, rare-disease medicine: here "the tech isn't strong enough" is true, and the adoption problem is secondary.</p>

<p>**High-stakes, high-precision settings.** Medicine, financial trading, and safety-critical systems impose hard requirements on explainability, reliability, and accountability. A physician's black-box distrust is a professional floor, not inertia. Watson for Oncology failed, in the first instance, because the technology didn't clear the reliability bar — not because the organization wouldn't adopt. In these settings the right order is: *clear the reliability threshold first, then do adoption engineering.*</p>

<p>**Organizations that can't even catch it — especially SMEs.** Some organizations aren't unwilling; they lack the baseline for adoption at all — no data infrastructure, no AI-capable staff, no budget or bandwidth for process redesign. The OECD–ICSB figures are stark: from 2023 to 2025, AI use among OECD small firms rose from 7% to 17.5%, medium firms from 13.6% to 29.5%, large firms from 30.4% to 52.1%. The small firms are "running fast and falling further behind." UK research adds the texture: skill gaps (cited by over 60% of firms), tool fragmentation, and uncertain ROI are the principal barriers.</p>

<p>For these organizations, "appoint an owner, train people, do the math" is empty advice. The right first move is to build the technical foundation and the talent baseline before anything else.</p>

<p>The rigorous version of the thesis, then, is worth saying in full: **"Adoption is a people problem" holds principally in the middle band — where the technology is already good enough but the organization hasn't caught it. At the two extremes — sparse data, high risk, thin organizational capability — "the tech isn't strong enough" or "the organization can't catch it" remains the real constraint.** Conceding that boundary is precisely what makes the main claim stronger everywhere else.</p>

<hr />

<h2>One global pattern, and one sharp divide</h2>

<p>Zoom out, and it becomes clear this is not a single country's quirk. It's a universal tension with a clean structural split.</p>

<p>**The shared pattern: technology spreads fast, organizations adapt slowly.** Lay three datasets side by side: McKinsey's "90% started, 25% succeeded, 75% stuck in pilots" (global); Prosci's "63% of AI implementation challenges stem from human factors, not technical limits" (global, 1,107 people, multiple industries); and Beike Finance's "89.84% deployed / 16.41% dedicated team / 47.66% untrained / 48.44% no ROI" (China, 18 industries).</p>

<p>The symmetry is the tell: the "deployment" numbers — how far the technology has spread — are uniformly high, while the "organizational adaptation" numbers — dedicated management, training, measurement — are uniformly low. Technology moves fast, organizations move slow, everywhere. This is not a feature of one market; it's the physics of AI adoption.</p>

<p>**The divide: large institutions vs. SMEs.** In the OECD figures, large firms' AI use (52.1%) is roughly three times small firms' (17.5%), and the gap is widening. The UK data sharpens it: SME adoption rose from 25% in 2024 to 54% by early 2026, but "strategic deployment with a clear business purpose" still sits around 16%, and only 12% of firms using AI report revenue growth attributable to it.</p>

<p>Thinking this through lands on a deeper point: among large institutions, "adoption deficit" shows up as *budget and tools, but no owner and no training*. Among small firms, it shows up as *no foundation to begin with*. The remedies differ accordingly — the former needs role redesign and change management, the latter needs a capability base first. Draw that distinction and the analysis stops pretending one prescription cures every patient.</p>

<hr />

<h2>The last hundred meters is a human problem</h2>

<p>Back to the opening misread.</p>

<p>The optimist keeps saying "just wait for the next version." But the models *have* been getting better, year after year — and the three-quarters of companies stuck in pilot didn't emerge on their own. Because between "the technology works" and "the technology is used" there was never an algorithm. There was an S-curve with no one assigned to climb it, a frontline that no one led across a trust gap, and a ledger no one dared to keep — or to cut.</p>

<p>**A new tool with no owner ends up as an old piece of furniture nobody ever claimed.**</p>

<p>If you're stuck somewhere in your own AI rollout, resist the urge to upgrade the model first. Look back at four simple questions first: is anyone accountable, can the frontline use it, has the process been redesigned, and does the math ever get done. The answer is almost never inside the tool.</p>

<p>Conceding that some settings are still waiting on the technology is exactly what gives you the standing to say, everywhere else, the harder thing:</p>

<p>**The problem isn't the AI. It's us — and that's fixable.**</p>

